// How long a solve took — and, just as importantly, WHEN WE DO NOT KNOW.
//
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// THE BUG THIS MODULE EXISTS TO KILL (2026-09-04)
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Game.jsx measured every solve with one line:
//
//     const deviceElapsedMs = isTimed(mode) ? Date.now() - dailyChallengeStartTime : 0;
//
// A practice round is not timed, so the `: 0` branch ran — and that zero was then
// written to `practice_solves.solve_time_seconds` as though it were a
// measurement. ALL 822 PRACTICE ROWS IN PRODUCTION carry it.
//
// Zero is not a slow value or a fast one. It is the FASTEST POSSIBLE VALUE, and
// every consumer that reads a solve time has to special-case it back out again:
// `craftStages.solveFacts` writes `0 means NEVER RECORDED` in its own doc comment
// and `divideFast` carries a defensive `f.seconds > 0` clause purely because of
// this. The consequence is that the Lightning Divide rung — "finish on a blue
// division, under the minute" — CANNOT LIGHT from practice, ever: the only rows
// that could satisfy it all claim to have taken no time at all.
//
// Writing 0 to mean "unknown" is the actual defect. The column is nullable
// (`solve_time_seconds double precision`, no NOT NULL), so the honest value was
// always available.
//
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// THE FIX, IN TWO HALVES
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//   1. MEASURE PRACTICE. A practice round has a real start — the moment the dice
//      land and the board becomes playable — so there is a real elapsed time to
//      record. Game.jsx stamps that moment; this module turns it into seconds.
//   2. WRITE `null` WHEN THERE IS GENUINELY NOTHING TO MEASURE. A board
//      rehydrated from `active_game` after the app was closed has no trustworthy
//      start (the player may have been away for a day), and a mode switch
//      invalidates the previous stamp. Those rows say `null` — "untimed" —
//      instead of claiming an instant solve.
//
// `null` and `0` are then no longer synonyms, which is what lets a reader
// distinguish "never measured" from "measured, and it really was that fast".
//
// Pure and clock-injectable, so the tests never depend on wall time.

/** What a solve time is when it was never measured. NOT zero — see the header. */
export const UNTIMED = null;

/**
 * Measure a solve from the moment its board became playable.
 *
 * @param {{startedAt?: number|null, now?: number}} args
 *   `startedAt` is an epoch-ms stamp, or null/undefined when unknown.
 * @returns {{deviceElapsedMs: number|null, solveTimeSeconds: number|null}}
 *   Both null when the start is unknown, so a caller cannot accidentally
 *   propagate a fabricated zero.
 */
export function measureSolve({ startedAt, now = Date.now() } = {}) {
  if (!Number.isFinite(startedAt) || /** @type {number} */ (startedAt) <= 0) {
    return { deviceElapsedMs: UNTIMED, solveTimeSeconds: UNTIMED };
  }
  if (!Number.isFinite(now)) {
    return { deviceElapsedMs: UNTIMED, solveTimeSeconds: UNTIMED };
  }
  // Clamped at 0 rather than allowed negative: a device clock stepped backwards
  // mid-round (NTP correction, manual change, a timezone rollover on some
  // Androids) would otherwise produce a negative "time" that reads as the
  // fastest solve in history on any `<` comparison.
  const deviceElapsedMs = Math.max(0, /** @type {number} */ (now) - /** @type {number} */ (startedAt));
  return { deviceElapsedMs, solveTimeSeconds: deviceElapsedMs / 1000 };
}

/**
 * The value to WRITE for a solve time: a real measurement, or `null`.
 *
 * Kept separate from {@link measureSolve} because it is the rule a persistence
 * call site needs, and stating it once is what stops the next writer reaching for
 * `?? 0`. A zero arriving from anywhere is treated as unknown, so legacy inputs
 * are normalised on the way in rather than being re-recorded.
 *
 * @param {number|null|undefined} solveTimeSeconds
 * @returns {number|null}
 */
export function recordableSolveTime(solveTimeSeconds) {
  return typeof solveTimeSeconds === 'number'
    && Number.isFinite(solveTimeSeconds)
    && solveTimeSeconds > 0
    ? solveTimeSeconds
    : UNTIMED;
}
