// The abbreviated legal lines the app shows alongside the promotion.
//
// ⚠️ ITS OWN FILE BECAUSE contest.js IS AT ITS CAP. The arch ratchet caps that
// module at 800 lines and these pushed it to 813. The convention says split
// rather than bless a bigger floor, and this is a clean seam: contest.js states
// what the promotion IS, this states what the LAW requires alongside it.
//
// FOUND BY AUDIT, 2026-09-17. US abbreviated sweepstakes rules are expected to
// state, wherever the promotion appears: NO PURCHASE NECESSARY, eligibility,
// dates, the sponsor, where to read the full rules — and these two. Every other
// item was already in the app; these were missing from every surface since the
// promotion launched.
//
// ⚠️ ABBREVIATED, NOT COMPLETE. These do not replace the official rules at
// CONTEST.rulesUrl, which is where the mechanics, winner selection and the
// state-specific terms live.

/**
 * ⚠️ THE LOAD-BEARING ONE. Sweepstakes law is STATE law, and this phrase is what
 * keeps an otherwise-lawful promotion from being an unlawful one in a
 * jurisdiction nobody considered. Three words.
 */
export const CONTEST_VOID = 'Void where prohibited';

/**
 * ⚠️ THIS MATTERS MORE SINCE 2026-09-17 THAN IT DID BEFORE. Entry used to go to
 * the fastest solver; now anyone who FINISHES a Daily is entered, so the week's
 * total is unbounded — however many entries every eligible player earns between
 * them.
 *
 * So a player reading "3 a day, 21 a week" with no odds statement can reasonably
 * take those numbers for their CHANCES rather than their entries. This is the
 * sentence that stops the cap being read as a probability.
 */
export const CONTEST_ODDS =
  'Odds of winning depend on the number of eligible entries that week.';

/**
 * WHAT ELSE WE PUT IN THEIR INBOX, BESIDES THE TWO PRIZE EMAILS.
 *
 * ⚠️ ADDED 2026-09-20 BECAUSE THE APP'S OWN POLICIES DID NOT PERMIT THE EMAIL WE
 * WERE ABOUT TO SEND. `CONTEST_CLAIM` says "two emails maximum", and that is
 * true — of PRIZE emails. It was the only sentence about email anywhere in the
 * app, so it read as the total. Meanwhile the entry-confirmation email (G in
 * the giveaway email set) goes to players who have not won anything, and
 * `public/privacy.html` and `public/terms.html` on the .app build disclosed no
 * such email at all; the .com copies did. Sending it would have contradicted
 * the policy the player actually agreed to.
 *
 * So this states the OTHER traffic: one entry confirmation a week at most, rule
 * and eligibility notices, and future promotions.
 *
 * The "if you win, we email you either way" clause is the load-bearing one. An
 * opt-out that a player fears could cost them $37 is not a real choice, and a
 * suppression list that swallowed a winner notice would be far worse than no
 * opt-out at all. `scripts/send-contest-email.mjs` checks the suppression list
 * for entrant email only; winner email is transactional and exempt.
 */
export const CONTEST_EMAILS =
  'At most one entry confirmation a week, plus occasional notes on rule changes, '
  + 'eligibility or future prize draws. Each has an opt-out. Opting out never affects '
  + 'entries — winners are emailed regardless.';
